The directory is empty, so I'll provide you the implementation pattern directly.

Here's how to set up global error handling with `IExceptionHandler` (**.NET 8+**):

**1. Create `Middleware/ApiExceptionHandler.cs`:**

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace YourApp.Middleware;

internal sealed class ApiExceptionHandler(ILogger<ApiExceptionHandler> logger)
    : IExceptionHandler
{
    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(
        HttpContext httpContext,
        Exception exception,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException => (StatusCodes.Status404NotFound, "Not Found"),
            ArgumentException   => (StatusCodes.Status400BadRequest, "Bad Request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (StatusCodes.Status409Conflict, "Conflict"),
            _ => (0, (string?)null)
        };

        if (statusCode == 0)
            return false; // fallback handler returns 500 with no details

        logger.LogWarning(exception, "Handled API exception: {Title}", title);

        httpContext.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;
        await httpContext.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(new ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = statusCode,
            Title = title,
            Detail = title,          // safe — never expose exception.Message
            Instance = httpContext.Request.Path
        }, cancellationToken);

        return true;
    }
}
```

**2. Register in `Program.cs`:**

```csharp
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<ApiExceptionHandler>();
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();

var app = builder.Build();

app.UseExceptionHandler();   // catches unhandled exceptions globally
app.UseStatusCodePages();    // returns ProblemDetails for bare status codes (e.g. 404)

// ... map your endpoints below
```

**How it works:**

- `IExceptionHandler` runs for every unhandled exception — no per-endpoint try-catch needed.
- Known exceptions get mapped to the appropriate status code with a safe `ProblemDetails` body.
- Unknown exceptions (`_ => (0, null)`) fall through to the built-in handler, which returns a generic 500 with no sensitive details (because `AddProblemDetails()` formats it as RFC 7807 automatically).
- `UseStatusCodePages()` ensures non-exception error paths (like `Results.NotFound()`) also get a Problem Details body.

**Example 500 response** (unhandled exception):
```json
{
  "type": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.6.1",
  "title": "An error occurred while processing your request.",
  "status": 500
}
```

No stack traces or internal messages are ever exposed.
